        <h3>Factory Profile #6: Mid-Size Full-Package Supplier (Best for brands scaling from test orders)</h3>
        <p>Balanced across fabric, cut-and-sew, and decoration, without the rigid minimums of a large exporter. Relaxed and oversized blocks are available, though fit refinement happens collaboratively, so expect a second fit sample on new silhouettes. Minimums are moderate with workable flexibility on colorways, which makes testing two or three silhouettes at once realistic.</p>
        <p><strong>Watch for / Best for:</strong> certification coverage varies widely here, which makes it worth checking against your sales channel requirements before artwork goes out. Suits brands in the scaling phase.</p>
        <h3>Factory Profile #7: Streetwear and Sportswear Crossover Factory</h3>
        <p>Comfortable building hoodies that blend streetwear silhouettes with technical fabrics and finishes, such as brushed poly-cotton blends or moisture-managed terry. Oversized fits are standard: boxy bodies and extended shoulders are daily work, not a special request. OEM and ODM both offered, with mid-range minimums.</p>
        <p><strong>Watch for / Best for:</strong> these plants often hold quality system certifications, so verify the scope actually covers apparel rather than a parent entity. Suits brands sitting between streetwear and activewear.</p>
        <h3>Factory Profile #8: Wash and Garment-Dye Specialist</h3>
        <p>Wash finishing is the differentiator here. Garment dye, pigment dye, acid wash, and vintage wash run as core processes rather than subcontracted extras, which is why this profile owns the vintage and pigment-dyed end of the category. Construction capability is solid, with one caveat worth taking seriously: wash processes move final dimensions, so your spec has to state post-wash measurements and shrinkage control becomes the critical variable. Wash development also consumes sampling rounds, so add time to the calendar. Colorfastness testing is non-negotiable for washed goods, and the reports should match the specific wash recipe you approved, not a neighboring one. Minimums are moderate.</p>
        <h3>Factory Profile #9: Low-MOQ Flexible Workshop</h3>
        <p>Small runs that larger factories turn down are the whole appeal, which makes this profile useful for validating a design before committing capital to volume. Basic oversized construction is available, but pattern sophistication is thin: bring detailed flat measurements and expect to drive the fit yourself. Unit cost runs higher per piece in exchange for the low threshold, and compliance documentation is limited enough that third-party testing usually has to be arranged independently. Best suited to founders running a first test order on a tight budget with a clear spec sheet.</p>
        <h3>Factory Profile #10: Full-Service Export House (Best for sourcing managed end to end)</h3>
        <p>Coordinates multiple factories, runs quality inspection, consolidates shipments, handles documentation. You are buying a service layer, not production capacity, so oversized capability depends entirely on the partner factory selected. Minimums follow whichever factory takes the job.</p>
        <p><strong>Watch for / Best for:</strong> compliance coordination comes with the service, but the underlying factory's certificates still need verifying, not just the export house's file. Suits buyers without an in-country team.</p>
        <p class="ar-note"><em>Comparing several of these profiles? <a href="/contact-us/">Get a Custom Quote</a> from YAUUM and benchmark it against your shortlist.</em></p>
        <h2 id="what-to-specify">Oversized vs. Regular Hoodies: What to Specify Before You Contact a Factory</h2>
        <p>Most sourcing friction traces back to a vague brief. Specify the parameters below and you get accurate quotes plus fewer sample revisions.</p>
        <ul class="ar-list"><li><strong>Fabric GSM.</strong> True oversized hoodies typically run 380–450 GSM fleece for a structured hand, while 280–340 GSM French terry drapes softer. State the target and a tolerance, usually ±5%.</li><li><strong>Drop-shoulder measurement.</strong> Give the distance from natural shoulder point to shoulder seam, commonly 4–10 cm. This one number drives the entire upper-body pattern.</li><li><strong>Body proportions.</strong> Chest width, body length, and sleeve length as flat measurements, not a size label. "Oversized" means four different things to four different factories.</li><li><strong>Fabric composition.</strong> Cotton/poly blends behave differently from 100% cotton on both shrinkage and print adhesion. Lock the ratio before sampling starts. If you are still deciding how much design ownership to keep, compare <a href="/our-services/">OEM vs ODM hoodie manufacturing</a> before you lock the spec.</li><li><strong>Wash and pre-shrink treatment.</strong> Decide between pre-shrunk and garment-washed, then set a maximum shrinkage allowance, typically 3–5% after the first home wash.</li><li><strong>Decoration method.</strong> Puff print behaves one way on light terry and another on dense fleece, so decoration choice constrains fabric choice.</li></ul>
        <p>For constructions that pair with these specs, see our <a href="/hoodie-manufacturer/#fabric-gsm">custom hoodie fabric options</a>.</p>
        <h2 id="how-to-vet">Step-by-Step: How to Vet &amp; Sample an Oversized Hoodie Factory</h2>
        <figure class="ar-figure">
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Step-by-step process of vetting and sampling an oversized hoodie factory">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">Required image</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Five-step vetting flow</strong><small>Needed: a verified process diagram matching the five steps in this section.</small></div>
      </div>
    </figure>
        <p><strong>Step 1. Send a structured inquiry.</strong> Include target GSM, drop-shoulder measurement, size range, order quantity, and decoration method. A factory that replies with specific questions has read your brief. One that fires back a generic price list has not.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 2. Request a lab dip and a fit sample.</strong> The fit sample is where oversized projects live or die. Measure it against your spec sheet with a tape, never by eye. Watch for three failures: shoulder seam landing at the natural shoulder, body width under your stated flat measurement, fabric lighter than the GSM you specified.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 3. Audit the factory.</strong> A live video walkthrough is enough for a first pass. Confirm the pattern room, cutting floor, and finishing lines exist and are running, then ask them to put your style's pattern block on camera. For a closer look, see <a href="/production/">our hoodie sampling process</a>.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 4. Run a small trial order.</strong> Produce a limited run in one or two colorways. Grading errors, decoration misalignment, and packaging problems surface here, at a cost you can absorb.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 5. Lock the pre-production sample.</strong> The approved PP sample becomes your quality reference. Keep a signed copy in hand and a photo record on file. Every shipment after that gets measured against it.</p>
        <p><strong>Red flags at any stage:</strong> refusing to share the pattern block, demanding a deposit before any physical sample, pushing you to skip the PP sample, and a quote sitting dramatically below everyone else on your shortlist. The last one usually means a fabric substitution you discover after shipment.</p>
        <h2 id="mistakes">Common Mistakes When Sourcing Oversized Hoodies from China</h2>
        <div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table"><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>Why It Hurts</th><th>How to Avoid It</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Comparing unit price only</td><td>Hides MOQ, sampling, and freight costs</td><td>Build a landed-cost comparison across all line items</td></tr><tr><td>Not confirming GSM in writing</td><td>"Heavyweight" is marketing, not a spec</td><td>State GSM with a ±5% tolerance in the tech pack</td></tr><tr><td>Skipping shrinkage testing</td><td>More fabric means amplified shrinkage</td><td>Request wash-test reports before bulk production</td></tr><tr><td>Approving fit from photos</td><td>Photos hide proportion errors</td><td>Measure a physical sample against your flat spec</td></tr><tr><td>One factory for every style</td><td>Silhouettes suit different strengths</td><td>Match the factory to the construction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
        <p>On the inspection side, run a structured <a href="/production/">hoodie quality control checklist</a> at pre-production, during production, and before shipment.</p>
        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <details class="ar-faq-item" open><summary><h3>What is the MOQ for custom oversized hoodies from China?</h3></summary><p>MOQ depends on the factory tier and your customization level. Flexible workshops and ODM studios commonly accept 100–300 pcs per style per color, while large exporters often require 500 pcs or more per color. Adding custom fabric, garment dye, or complex decoration usually raises the minimum. Confirm MOQ per style and per color, not as a single order figure.</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How much does it cost to manufacture an oversized hoodie in China?</h3></summary><p>FOB pricing for a custom oversized hoodie commonly falls in the $8–22 per piece range, driven by fabric GSM, decoration complexity, order quantity, and finishing. A basic 380 GSM fleece pullover with one-color print sits at the lower end; garment-dyed or heavily embroidered pieces sit higher. Use these ranges for planning rather than as a quote.</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>Can Chinese factories produce true oversized / drop-shoulder fits?</h3></summary><p>Yes, but capability varies sharply. Factories with existing oversized pattern blocks produce accurate drop-shoulder geometry on the first or second sample. Factories that grade a regular-fit block outward often deliver a garment that is wider but not correctly proportioned. Always request a physical fit sample and measure the drop-shoulder distance against your spec.</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How long does sampling and production take?</h3></summary><p>Sampling commonly runs 7–15 working days per stage, covering lab dip, fit sample, and pre-production sample. Bulk production typically takes 25–45 days after sample approval, with peak season adding time. Ocean freight adds roughly 25–35 days depending on destination.</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>What certifications should I look for in a hoodie manufacturer?</h3></summary><p>The relevant set depends on your market and sales channel. Social compliance certifications are frequently required by large retailers, and quality management certifications support consistency claims. For washed or printed goods, request colorfastness and decoration-durability test reports. Always verify the certificate scope and expiry date rather than accepting a logo on a website.</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>Can I get custom labels, tags, and packaging (private label)?</h3></summary><p>Yes. Private label is standard across most factories in this tier. Typical scope includes woven or printed main labels, care labels, hang tags, and polybag or box packaging. Confirm artwork formats, minimum quantities for custom tags, and whether labeling is done in-house or outsourced, since outsourcing adds lead time.</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How do I verify a factory is legitimate before ordering?</h3></summary><p>Ask for a live video walkthrough of the production floor, request the pattern block for your style, and start with a paid sample before any bulk deposit. Cross-check the business license against the company name on the invoice. A factory that resists a physical sample or a live walkthrough is a risk regardless of how competitive the quote looks.</p></details>
        <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>What payment terms are typical for hoodie manufacturing orders?</h3></summary><p>Common structures include a deposit of around 30% with the balance before shipment, or against a copy of the bill of lading for established relationships. Some factories offer letter of credit terms for larger orders. Terms are negotiable and typically improve as order history builds. Confirm currency, bank details, and any inspection-linked payment conditions in writing.</p></details>
        <h2 id="next-step">Next Step: Get a Custom Oversized Hoodie Quote</h2>
        <p>You now have a framework that works on any factory in your pipeline: verify the oversized pattern block, put GSM and shrinkage limits in writing, measure a physical sample, lock a pre-production standard before bulk starts. That discipline decides your outcome far more than which name sits at the top of any list.</p>
